{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the complex web of relationships, ambition, and betrayal that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a modern tragedy wrapped in the sleek aesthetics of digital age capitalism. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached, a genius haunted by rejection and driven by a need to prove himself. his portrayal is chillingly accurate, capturing the quiet intensity and cold logic that fueled facebook’s rise.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a tour de force—fast-paced, witty, and layered with dialogue that crackles with intellectual energy. every line feels purposeful, every exchange a step in a legal or emotional chess game. the film’s structure, framed by two lawsuits—one from the winklevoss twins and one from co-founder eduardo saverin—creates a compelling narrative rhythm that alternates between courtroom drama and flashbacks, revealing the gradual unraveling of friendships and ideals.\n\njoseph gordon-levitt is superb as eduardo saverin, the loyal friend turned adversary, his growing disillusionment palpable and heartbreaking. armie hammer, in a dual role as the winklevoss twins, uses motion capture and digital effects to astonishing effect, making the twins feel like two sides of the same coin—privileged, entitled, and tragically out of step with the new world they helped inspire.\n\nthe score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and minimalist, a blend of electronic pulses and melancholic strings that perfectly mirrors the isolation and urgency of the story. the cinematography, with its cool tones and tight framing, reinforces the film’s tone: clinical, detached, and relentless.\n\nbeneath the surface, the film is less about facebook and more about the cost of innovation—the erosion of trust, the commodification of connection, and the loneliness that can come with unprecedented success. it asks uncomfortable questions: was zuckerberg a visionary or a thief? did he build a platform for the world, or just for himself?\n\nthe social network doesn’t offer easy answers. instead, it leaves you with a sense of unease, a quiet awe at how something born from a college dorm room changed the world—and how little it cost the people who built it to change it for the worse. it’s a film that feels more relevant with each passing year, a chilling portrait of ambition in the digital era. brilliant, sharp, and unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014733182615600526}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005539313424378633}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.2500340938568115}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.4748547077178955}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009525052737444639}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003316618502140045}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0001037067049765028}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.3480844497680664}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.006731452886015177}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.5061202645301819}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.69933797325939e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0002890407631639391}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.7635160088539124}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -7.033323527139146e-06}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.4804927110671997}, {"token": " web", "logprob": -0.804498016834259}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " relationships", "logprob": -0